              "text": "In the precious metals and jewelry industries, \"clean gold\" refers to highly refined, 24-karat gold. In commercial markets, this standard is set at **99.9% purity** (often referred to as \".999 fine\" or \"three nines fine\"). \n\nAchieving a purity of exactly 100% is physically, chemically, and thermodynamically impossible. The 0.1% of trace impurities remain for several fundamental reasons:\n\n### 1. The Laws of Thermodynamics (Entropy of Mixing)\nAt a molecular level, you are fighting physics. According to the Second Law of Thermodynamics, systems naturally progress toward disorder (entropy). \nWhen you try to separate two different types of atoms (such as gold from trace silver, copper, or lead), you are fighting the **entropy of mixing**. As the concentration of impurities in the gold approaches zero, the thermodynamic energy required to remove the remaining few \"stray\" atoms increases exponentially. To strip away every single non-gold atom among quadrillions of gold atoms would theoretically require an infinite amount of energy and time.\n\n### 2. Limits of Industrial Refining Processes\nGold is purified using two main industrial methods, both of which have practical chemical limitations:\n* **The Miller Process:** This pyrometallurgical method bubbles chlorine gas through molten gold. The chlorine bonds with impurities (like silver and copper) to form salts that float to the top. This process maxes out at **99.5% purity**.\n* **The Wohlwill Process:** To get gold cleaner, refiners use this electrochemical process, which dissolves the gold in an acid bath and electroplates it onto a cathode. This can push purity to **99.9%** or **99.99%** (four nines fine). However, metals with very similar electrochemical properties to gold (like platinum, palladium, or silver) are incredibly difficult to separate completely and will always leave trace molecules behind.\n\n### 3. Contamination from the Environment and Tools\nDuring the final stages of refining, gold must be melted and poured into molds. Even in a highly controlled, sterile environment, the molten gold comes into contact with:\n* The air (introducing micro-particles of carbon, oxygen, or nitrogen).\n* The refining crucibles (made of graphite, clay, or silica), which shed trace atoms into the liquid metal.\n* The tools used to stir, pour, and stamp the gold.\nBecause gold is an excellent solvent when melted, it easily absorbs these tiny environmental contaminants, preventing it from ever remaining 100% pure.\n\n### 4. Scientific Limits of Measurement (Assaying)\nEven if a scientist somehow managed to isolate a single, theoretically perfect crystal of 100% gold, **they could never prove it**. \nAssaying technologies (such as Fire Assay, X-ray Fluorescence, or Mass Spectrometry) have a limit of detection. Because instruments cannot prove the absolute *absence* of all other elements, claiming a bar is \"100% pure\" is legally and scientifically fraudulent. Specifying 99.9% or 99.99% is the highest certifiable limit that standard laboratory equipment can guarantee.\n\nFor all practical, industrial, and financial purposes, **99.9% is considered completely pure gold**. It behaves exactly like pure gold, displays the classic rich yellow luster, and is as \"clean\" as physical science allows.",
