              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 99,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "In the precious metals and jewelry industries, \"clean gold\" refers to highly refined, 24-karat gold"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 189,
                "end_index": 221,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "999 fine\" or \"three nines fine\")"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 225,
                "end_index": 319,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Achieving a purity of exactly 100% is physically, chemically, and thermodynamically impossible"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 398,
                "end_index": 491,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Laws of Thermodynamics (Entropy of Mixing)\nAt a molecular level, you are fighting physics"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 493,
                "end_index": 592,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "According to the Second Law of Thermodynamics, systems naturally progress toward disorder (entropy)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 595,
                "end_index": 742,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When you try to separate two different types of atoms (such as gold from trace silver, copper, or lead), you are fighting the **entropy of mixing**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 744,
                "end_index": 907,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "As the concentration of impurities in the gold approaches zero, the thermodynamic energy required to remove the remaining few \"stray\" atoms increases exponentially"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 909,
                "end_index": 1048,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To strip away every single non-gold atom among quadrillions of gold atoms would theoretically require an infinite amount of energy and time"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1058,
                "end_index": 1297,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Limits of Industrial Refining Processes\nGold is purified using two main industrial methods, both of which have practical chemical limitations:\n* **The Miller Process:** This pyrometallurgical method bubbles chlorine gas through molten gold"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1299,
                "end_index": 1394,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The chlorine bonds with impurities (like silver and copper) to form salts that float to the top"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1396,
                "end_index": 1438,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This process maxes out at **99.5% purity**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1440,
                "end_index": 1608,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Wohlwill Process:** To get gold cleaner, refiners use this electrochemical process, which dissolves the gold in an acid bath and electroplates it onto a cathode"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1610,
                "end_index": 1675,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This can push purity to **99.9%** or **99.99%** (four nines fine)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1886,
                "end_index": 2009,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Contamination from the Environment and Tools\nDuring the final stages of refining, gold must be melted and poured into molds"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2011,
                "end_index": 2172,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Even in a highly controlled, sterile environment, the molten gold comes into contact with:\n* The air (introducing micro-particles of carbon, oxygen, or nitrogen)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2174,
                "end_index": 2280,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* The refining crucibles (made of graphite, clay, or silica), which shed trace atoms into the liquid metal"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2282,
                "end_index": 2332,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* The tools used to stir, pour, and stamp the gold"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                15,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2334,
                "end_index": 2484,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because gold is an excellent solvent when melted, it easily absorbs these tiny environmental contaminants, preventing it from ever remaining 100% pure"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2494,
                "end_index": 2668,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Scientific Limits of Measurement (Assaying)\nEven if a scientist somehow managed to isolate a single, theoretically perfect crystal of 100% gold, **they could never prove it**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2671,
                "end_index": 2781,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Assaying technologies (such as Fire Assay, X-ray Fluorescence, or Mass Spectrometry) have a limit of detection"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2783,
                "end_index": 2932,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because instruments cannot prove the absolute *absence* of all other elements, claiming a bar is \"100% pure\" is legally and scientifically fraudulent"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2934,
                "end_index": 3042,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Specifying 99.9% or 99.99% is the highest certifiable limit that standard laboratory equipment can guarantee"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                16
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3045,
                "end_index": 3144,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "For all practical, industrial, and financial purposes, **99.9% is considered completely pure gold**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3146,
                "end_index": 3266,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It behaves exactly like pure gold, displays the classic rich yellow luster, and is as \"clean\" as physical science allows"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
